{"name":"Lilting Laughter","hex":"#fcebd8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lilting","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a lilt; with energy, spirit and sprightliness; lively and cheerful."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of lively singing without any musical accompaniment, where the tune itself is sung, often to nonsense syllables."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lilting"},{"term":"laughter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The sound of laughing, produced by air so expelled; any similar sound.","A movement (usually involuntary) of the muscles of the laughing face, particularly of the lips, and of the whole body, with a peculiar expression of the eyes, indicating merriment, satisfaction or derision, and usually attended by a sonorous and interrupted expulsion of air from the lungs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/laughter"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lilting","blocks":[{"text":"Lilting is a form of traditional singing common in the Goidelic speaking areas of Ireland, Scotland and the Isle of Man, though singing styles like it occur in many other countries. It goes under many names, and is sometimes referred to as diddling, mouth music, jigging, chin music or cheek music, puirt à beul or canntaireachd in Scottish Gaelic, or portaireacht bhéil in Irish. The syllables sung do not require any one-to-one corresponding note, and in this way it resembles scat singing, and is distinguished from the singing of sol-fa or sargam."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lilting","wikidata":"Q3240930"},{"title":"Laughter","blocks":[{"text":"Laughter is a typically pleasant physical reaction and emotion consisting usually of rhythmical, usually audible contractions of the diaphragm and other parts of the respiratory system. It is a response to certain external or internal stimuli. Laughter can rise from such stimuli as being tickled, or from humorous stories, imagery, videos or thoughts. Most commonly, it is considered an auditory expression of a number of positive emotional states, such as joy, mirth, happiness or relief. On some occasions, however, it may be caused by contrary emotional states such as embarrassment, surprise, or confusion such as nervous laughter or courtesy laugh. Age, gender, education, language and culture are all indicators as to whether a person will experience laughter in a given situation. Other than humans, some other species of primate express laughter-like vocalizations in response to physical contact such as wrestling, play chasing or tickling."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Laughing_boy_at_golden_hour.jpg/330px-Laughing_boy_at_golden_hour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laughter","wikidata":"Q170579"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg/500px-Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg/1280px-Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg/500px-Human%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A young girl from Laos laughing while hugging her dog","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHuman%E2%80%93canine_friendship_-_smiling_girl_hugging_her_dog_at_golden_hour_in_Laos.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Laughing_woman.jpg/500px-Laughing_woman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Laughing_woman.jpg/1280px-Laughing_woman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Laughing_woman.jpg/500px-Laughing_woman.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A woman laughing","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALaughing_woman.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Laughing_Nyahsa_GalawebDesign.jpg/500px-Laughing_Nyahsa_GalawebDesign.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Laughing_Nyahsa_GalawebDesign.jpg/500px-Laughing_Nyahsa_GalawebDesign.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A man laughing","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALaughing_Nyahsa_GalawebDesign.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Laughter_by_David_Shankbone.jpg/500px-Laughter_by_David_Shankbone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Laughter_by_David_Shankbone.jpg/1280px-Laughter_by_David_Shankbone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Laughter_by_David_Shankbone.jpg/500px-Laughter_by_David_Shankbone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Laughter is a common response to tickling.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALaughter_by_David_Shankbone.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Hans_von_Aachen_-_Two_Laughing_Men_%28Self-portrait%29.jpg/500px-Hans_von_Aachen_-_Two_Laughing_Men_%28Self-portrait%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Hans_von_Aachen_-_Two_Laughing_Men_%28Self-portrait%29.jpg/500px-Hans_von_Aachen_-_Two_Laughing_Men_%28Self-portrait%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two laughing men by Hans von Aachen, circa 1574","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHans_von_Aachen_-_Two_Laughing_Men_(Self-portrait).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Laughter_Yoga.jpg/500px-Laughter_Yoga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Laughter_Yoga.jpg/1280px-Laughter_Yoga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Laughter_Yoga.jpg/500px-Laughter_Yoga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Laughter therapy/yoga","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALaughter_Yoga.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/AdvertYoungsTheHatterVictorianEraBridgeportCT.jpg/500px-AdvertYoungsTheHatterVictorianEraBridgeportCT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/AdvertYoungsTheHatterVictorianEraBridgeportCT.jpg/500px-AdvertYoungsTheHatterVictorianEraBridgeportCT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Late 19th-century or early 20th-century depiction of different stages of laughter on advertising cards","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdvertYoungsTheHatterVictorianEraBridgeportCT.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://t